Chapter: LiteralSubpattern
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 Literal patterns must be tested, whether they stand alone or sit
 inside a constructor pattern.

 Two silent miscompiles are pinned here, both found 2026-07-20 and both
 of the worst kind: they compiled clean, raised no diagnostic, and
 returned a confident wrong answer.

 The first: a literal nested in a constructor pattern was never
 compared. `bind-ctor-fields` bound variable sub-patterns and dropped
 everything else, and `EmitPatternResult` carried one patch slot so a
 second test had nowhere to be reported. Every arm whose CONSTRUCTOR
 matched won, so `is BInt (0)` matched any BInt, and a four-way
 operator dispatch collapsed onto whichever arm came first. In the tree
 this was apps/cvmm/Calculator treating every operator as plus and
 apps/browser/PageCompiler taking the then-branch of every conditional.

 The second: a Text literal pattern compared a pointer against
 `pat-lit-to-integer` of its own spelling, so `is "sin" ->` matched
 nothing at any nesting depth.

 Every case below must therefore reach a DIFFERENT arm. A test where
 the arms return the same value could not tell either bug from a
 working compiler.

Section: Types

  Box =
    | BInt (Integer)
    | BText (Text)
    | BFlag (Boolean)
    | BPair (Integer) (Text)
    | BNone

Section: Literal Inside A Constructor

  by-int : Box -> Integer
  by-int (b) = when b
    is BInt (0) -> 10
    is BInt (1) -> 11
    is BInt (7) -> 17
    is BInt (n) -> 19
    is otherwise -> 99

  by-text : Box -> Integer
  by-text (b) = when b
    is BText ("sin") -> 20
    is BText ("cos") -> 21
    is BText (s) -> 29
    is otherwise -> 99

  by-flag : Box -> Integer
  by-flag (b) = when b
    is BFlag (True) -> 30
    is BFlag (False) -> 31
    is otherwise -> 99

 A literal beside a binder, and two literals in one pattern. The binder
 must still bind while the literal is tested.

  by-pair : Box -> Integer
  by-pair (b) = when b
    is BPair (0) (s) -> 40
    is BPair (n) ("hit") -> 41
    is BPair (n) (s) -> 42
    is otherwise -> 99

Section: Literal Standing Alone

 The same two questions without a constructor around them. Integer
 literals here always worked; Text literals never did.

  bare-int : Integer -> Integer
  bare-int (n) = when n
    is 0 -> 50
    is 1 -> 51
    is otherwise -> 59

  bare-text : Text -> Integer
  bare-text (t) = when t
    is "sin" -> 60
    is "cos" -> 61
    is otherwise -> 69

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni ("int-0 " & show (by-int (BInt 0)))
    print-line-uni ("int-1 " & show (by-int (BInt 1)))
    print-line-uni ("int-7 " & show (by-int (BInt 7)))
    print-line-uni ("int-n " & show (by-int (BInt 3)))
    print-line-uni ("txt-sin " & show (by-text (BText "sin")))
    print-line-uni ("txt-cos " & show (by-text (BText "cos")))
    print-line-uni ("txt-oth " & show (by-text (BText "zzz")))
    print-line-uni ("flag-t " & show (by-flag (BFlag True)))
    print-line-uni ("flag-f " & show (by-flag (BFlag False)))
    print-line-uni ("pair-0 " & show (by-pair (BPair 0 "x")))
    print-line-uni ("pair-hit " & show (by-pair (BPair 5 "hit")))
    print-line-uni ("pair-oth " & show (by-pair (BPair 5 "x")))
    print-line-uni ("bare-0 " & show (bare-int 0))
    print-line-uni ("bare-1 " & show (bare-int 1))
    print-line-uni ("bare-n " & show (bare-int 4))
    print-line-uni ("bare-sin " & show (bare-text "sin"))
    print-line-uni ("bare-cos " & show (bare-text "cos"))
    print-line-uni ("bare-oth " & show (bare-text "zzz"))
    print-line-uni "done"
  end
